-- title:
--   Theorem 5.1 — a size-independent tester for Slater determinants ($\beta=\sqrt{12}$, two copies per round)
-- statement:
--   Let $\beta=\sqrt{12}$. There is a constant $A>0$ such that for every number of modes $n$, all $0\le\varepsilon_1<\varepsilon_2<1$ with $\varepsilon_2>\beta\varepsilon_1$ and all $0<\delta<1/2$, there is an $(\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2,\delta)$-tester for the class $\mathcal S$ of Slater determinants that acts on two copies at a time and uses
--
--   $$
--   t\ \le\ A\,\frac{\log(1/\delta)}{(\varepsilon_2-\beta\varepsilon_1)^2}
--   $$
--
--   copies in total. The tester must be correct for every pure state of the full $n$-mode Fock space.
--
--   This is the clause of Theorem 1.4 for Slater determinants, with the explicit constant $\beta$ that the proof produces.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: a t-copy (ε₁, ε₂, δ)-tester acting on k copies of the state at a time → a BlockTester: one two-outcome measurement {E, I − E} on ℋ^{⊗k} repeated in independent rounds, a deterministic decision on the outcomes, t = k · rounds; reason: this makes 'acting on k copies at a time' precise (Table 1, Algorithm 1); every such procedure is a tester in the sense of Definition 1.2
--   - reading: an O(log(1/δ)/(ε₂ − βε₁)²)-copy tester → one constant A > 0, chosen before the number of modes n and before δ, ε₁, ε₂, with t ≤ A · log(1/δ)/(ε₂ − βε₁)²; reason: the bound is size independent (p.5: independent of n and of any energy bound)
--   - encoding: the fermionic Fock space ⋀ℂⁿ and the bosonic Fock space ⋁ℂⁿ → ℓ²(Fin n → Fin 2) (Jordan–Wigner, 1 = occupied, 0-based modes) and ℓ²(Fin n → ℕ); ℋ^{⊗k} is ℓ²(Fin k → ι); reason: unitarily the same spaces; Lean convention
--   - encoding: classes of pure states (rays) → sets of unit vectors closed under global phase (Coh_n gets an explicit e^{iθ}, G_f an explicit phase in its orbit); reason: dist(ψ, 𝒞) is phase-invariant, so no distance changes
--   - encoding: fermionic Gaussian unitaries, generated by Hamiltonians quadratic in c, c† (p.17) → finite products exp(A₁)⋯exp(A_L) with A_l = ¼ Σ_{p,q} h⁽ˡ⁾_{pq} γ_p γ_q, h⁽ˡ⁾ real antisymmetric (Gf, Slater ⊆ Gf); reason: a Hermitian quadratic Hamiltonian is (i/4) Σ h_{pq} γ_p γ_q + c·I with h real antisymmetric, so the group it generates is exactly these products up to the phase e^{−ic}
-- source:
--   Iyer, O'Gorman, Parekh, Thompson, Zhao, Optimal testing of fermionic and bosonic Gaussian states, arXiv:2609.39113 (sha256 6683e57b…), Theorem 5.1, p.41

import Definitions.Def_GaussTesting_Slater
import Definitions.Def_GaussTesting_IsTester

namespace GaussTesting

/-- **Theorem 5.1.** Let `β = √12`. For all `0 ≤ ε₁ < ε₂ < 1` with `ε₂ > βε₁` and `0 < δ < 1/2`, there is an
`O(log(1/δ)/(ε₂−βε₁)²)`-copy `(ε₁, ε₂, δ)`-tester for Slater determinants. The tester acts on two copies at a time.

Source: Iyer, O'Gorman, Parekh, Thompson, Zhao, *Optimal testing of fermionic and bosonic Gaussian states*, arXiv:2609.39113 (sha256 6683e57b…), Theorem 5.1, p.41.

**Formalization Note.** "There is an `O(log(1/δ)/(ε₂−βε₁)²)`-copy tester" is pinned as: one constant
`A > 0`, chosen before the number of modes `n` and before `δ, ε₁, ε₂`, such that the tester uses
`t = k · rounds ≤ A · log(1/δ)/(ε₂ − βε₁)²` copies (size independence, Theorem 1.4, p.5). The tester
measures blocks of `k` copies (`BlockTester _ k`) and must work for every unit vector of the full Fock
space. -/
theorem theorem_5_1 :
    ∃ A : ℝ, 0 < A ∧ ∀ (n : ℕ) (δ ε₁ ε₂ : ℝ), 0 ≤ ε₁ → ε₁ < ε₂ → ε₂ < 1 → Real.sqrt 12 * ε₁ < ε₂ →
      0 < δ → δ < 1 / 2 →
      ∃ T : BlockTester (FermiCap.Gaussian.Qubits n) 2, IsTester (Slater n) T ε₁ ε₂ δ ∧
        ((2 * T.rounds : ℕ) : ℝ) ≤ A * (Real.log (1 / δ) / (ε₂ - Real.sqrt 12 * ε₁) ^ 2) := by sorry

end GaussTesting
